      "finality_policy": "confirmation_depth"
    },
    "episode_id": "case-cb4848dd6210-0002",
    "evidence": "Challenge to the claim 'The 520 total due on invoice INV-2207 equals the sum of its line items.'\n\nFrom the invoice record (INV-2207):\n- Line 1, Pallet wrap: quantity 3 x unit price 120 = 360 (matches the stated line total of 360).\n- Line 2, Corner boards: quantity 2 x unit price 45 = 90 (matches the stated line total of 90).\n- Sum of the line items: 360 + 90 = 450.\n- Header total due: 520.\n\n520 is not equal to 450. The header total exceeds the sum of the line items by 70 (520 - 450 = 70).\n\nThe invoice record states: 'No taxes, fees, or other lines appear on the invoice.' There is therefore no additional line, tax, fee, or other charge in the record that could reconcile the header total to the line items. Both line totals are internally consistent with their own quantity x unit price, so the discrepancy is not an artifact of a mis-stated line: the itemised content of the invoice simply sums to 450 while the header asserts 520.\n\nThe claim under review is an assertion of arithmetic equality between the header total and the sum of the line items. On the invoice record that equality does not hold, so the claim is false as stated. I acknowledge the limit of this evidence: it establishes that the invoice's own figures are internally inconsistent, not why the invoice was prepared that way. That question does not affect whether the stated equality is true.",
    "evidence_hash": "0xe424eba3bde79d0788e6c2a8e928a8813ff16f8c9e4b357432a79a1fd8351171",
    "juror_bond_wei": "100000",
